WEBVTT

00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:09.272
Ares IVto Kumagawa.

00:00:09.342 --> 00:00:11.503
How was the sunrise
from down there?

00:00:11.578 --> 00:00:12.943
Beautiful.

00:00:13.013 --> 00:00:15.743
There was a little green
mixed in today.

00:00:15.815 --> 00:00:18.215
It was really quite spectacular.

00:00:18.284 --> 00:00:20.343
Sorry I missed it.

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:22.047
Next time, Lieutenant.

00:00:22.122 --> 00:00:24.556
Any luck drilling
through that lava plain?

00:00:24.624 --> 00:00:25.886
Roger that.

00:00:25.959 --> 00:00:27.586
We broke through
the iron oxide barrier,

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:28.922
and we're down to eight meters.

00:00:28.995 --> 00:00:31.122
We should have some samples

00:00:31.197 --> 00:00:32.926
by the end of the day
if all goes well.

00:00:32.999 --> 00:00:34.660
On to more important matters.

00:00:34.734 --> 00:00:36.463
Received an uplink from Houston.

00:00:36.536 --> 00:00:38.868
The Yankees won game five
of the World Series.

00:00:38.938 --> 00:00:40.200
You owe me 20 bucks.

00:00:40.273 --> 00:00:41.240
Eh, it's not over yet.

00:00:41.307 --> 00:00:42.535
There's still game six.

00:00:42.609 --> 00:00:44.975
We're not the only ones
making history today.

00:00:45.078 --> 00:00:46.943
Bokai just broke
DiMaggio's record.

00:00:47.013 --> 00:00:48.173
I don't believe it.

00:00:48.248 --> 00:00:49.408
If you ask me,

00:00:49.482 --> 00:00:51.177
Houston's been manipulating
the stats.

00:00:51.251 --> 00:00:54.220
They do have a few too many
Kings fans at Mission Control.

00:00:54.287 --> 00:00:55.652
Well, how else do you explain

00:00:55.722 --> 00:00:57.656
Bokai beating out
the Yankee Clipper?

00:00:59.993 --> 00:01:02.359
Commander, we lost you
for a second there.

00:01:02.429 --> 00:01:04.192
The ride got a little bumpy.

00:01:04.264 --> 00:01:05.458
Stand by.

00:01:05.532 --> 00:01:08.160
I'm picking up some turbulence.

00:01:08.234 --> 00:01:09.496
At your altitude?

00:01:09.569 --> 00:01:11.400
Checking LIDAR.

00:01:11.471 --> 00:01:13.200
What the hell?

00:01:13.273 --> 00:01:14.831
John, what's going on?

00:01:14.908 --> 00:01:17.274
There's an object
closing on my position...

00:01:17.343 --> 00:01:20.335
azimuth 121.6.

00:01:20.413 --> 00:01:22.142
Whatever it is, it's huge.

00:01:22.215 --> 00:01:23.580
It's over 1,000 meters across.

00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:26.585
How could something that big
appear out of nowhere?

00:01:26.653 --> 00:01:29.451
I don't know,
but I'm getting out of its way.

00:01:29.522 --> 00:01:30.819
Firing thrusters.

00:01:32.325 --> 00:01:34.384
It's probably
just a solar flare.

00:01:34.461 --> 00:01:36.759
Let's hope so.

00:01:45.538 --> 00:01:47.563
It's no solar flare.

00:03:44.123 --> 00:03:45.556
Come in.

00:03:47.293 --> 00:03:49.261
Come in.

00:04:05.578 --> 00:04:07.512
Chakotay to the Bridge.

00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:09.775
This is Transporter Room 2.

00:04:09.849 --> 00:04:11.646
I'm trying to reach the Bridge.

00:04:11.718 --> 00:04:14.346
Your com signal
was routed here, sir.

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:16.445
Can you patch me through?

00:04:16.522 --> 00:04:18.717
Uh, pardon me? Who is this?

00:04:18.791 --> 00:04:19.723
Patch you through where?

00:04:19.792 --> 00:04:21.089
Patch you through
where... where?

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:22.525
Neelix?

00:04:22.595 --> 00:04:23.527
Commander?

00:04:23.596 --> 00:04:25.496
Double check
on the fuel generators.

00:04:25.565 --> 00:04:27.499
Control seven, return to base...

00:04:27.567 --> 00:04:28.898
Maintenance Team 49...

00:04:28.968 --> 00:04:30.162
Culhane to Nozawa,
what's keeping you?

00:04:30.236 --> 00:04:31.396
Computer, locate Naomi...

00:04:31.471 --> 00:04:32.938
We're meeting
in the Briefing Room.

00:04:33.039 --> 00:04:34.563
I can't get through
to Engineering.

00:04:34.641 --> 00:04:36.040
I'm scheduled for Holodeck 2.

00:04:36.109 --> 00:04:38.339
Culhane to Nozawa...

00:05:01.567 --> 00:05:04.263
I see you're making some changes
to the computer core.

00:05:04.337 --> 00:05:06.532
I have enhanced
the command sequencers

00:05:06.606 --> 00:05:07.903
with Borg algorithms.

00:05:08.007 --> 00:05:10.100
Well, your enhancements
are wreaking havoc

00:05:10.176 --> 00:05:11.575
with our secondary systems.

00:05:11.644 --> 00:05:12.906
Insignificant malfunctions.

00:05:13.046 --> 00:05:14.308
I will correct them.

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:16.371
I don't recall authorizing
any modifications.

00:05:16.449 --> 00:05:17.916
The computer core
is inefficient.

00:05:17.984 --> 00:05:19.383
It needed to be improved.

00:05:19.452 --> 00:05:22.649
I appreciate your initiative,
but that's not up to you.

00:05:22.722 --> 00:05:24.849
I've explained the value
of these enhancements

00:05:24.924 --> 00:05:26.016
on several occasions,

00:05:26.092 --> 00:05:28.287
but Lieutenant Torres
chose to ignore me.

00:05:28.361 --> 00:05:30.921
I thought a demonstration
would be more persuasive.

00:05:31.064 --> 00:05:33.828
Well, I doubt this one's
going to change her mind.

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:36.061
Clearly Voyager
is not yet ready

00:05:36.135 --> 00:05:37.363
for assimilation.

00:05:39.572 --> 00:05:41.062
A joke.

00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:42.402
The Doctor suggested

00:05:42.475 --> 00:05:44.739
that I diffuse tense
situations with humor.

00:05:44.811 --> 00:05:45.937
Good idea.

00:05:46.012 --> 00:05:48.139
Now, how about getting
these systems back up again

00:05:48.214 --> 00:05:49.442
so I can get back
to my book?

00:05:53.186 --> 00:05:56.917
We're experiencing a
shipwide power drain.

00:05:56.989 --> 00:06:00.049
This is unrelated
to my modifications.

00:06:02.128 --> 00:06:04.858
Senior officers to the Bridge.

00:06:09.902 --> 00:06:12.132
It's 2:00 in the morning,
Ensign.

00:06:12.205 --> 00:06:14.867
This better be more
than a little turbulence.

00:06:14.941 --> 00:06:16.340
You won't be disappointed.

00:06:16.409 --> 00:06:18.070
We've got level-9
gravimetric distortions

00:06:18.144 --> 00:06:19.236
closing on our position.

00:06:19.312 --> 00:06:20.904
They are emanating
from subspace.

00:06:20.980 --> 00:06:22.914
On screen.

00:06:25.918 --> 00:06:27.317
Shields.

00:06:31.958 --> 00:06:33.619
It's heading right toward us.

00:06:33.693 --> 00:06:35.524
Evasive maneuvers.

00:06:44.103 --> 00:06:45.161
Captain?

00:06:45.238 --> 00:06:47.365
Say good morning
to 30 million terajoules

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:48.429
of subspace energy.

00:06:48.508 --> 00:06:50.032
This thing is following us.

00:06:50.109 --> 00:06:51.940
I can't outrun it at impulse.

00:06:52.011 --> 00:06:52.978
Go to warp.

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:56.880
It's disrupting our warp field.

00:06:56.949 --> 00:06:58.746
If it any gets closer,
the gravimetric stresses

00:06:58.818 --> 00:07:00.410
are going to rip the plating
off our hull.

00:07:00.486 --> 00:07:02.386
Captain, I recognize
this phenomenon.

00:07:02.455 --> 00:07:04.719
Its Borg designation
is Spatial Anomaly 521.

00:07:04.791 --> 00:07:08.318
It's attracted to objects that
emit electromagnetic energy.

00:07:08.394 --> 00:07:11.886
We have to cut power
and reverse our shield polarity.

00:07:11.998 --> 00:07:13.625
Do it.

00:07:21.107 --> 00:07:22.768
That was close.

00:07:24.110 --> 00:07:26.374
I recognize this anomaly, too.

00:07:26.446 --> 00:07:29.313
It's called a graviton ellipse.

00:07:29.382 --> 00:07:31.475
According to
the Federation database,

00:07:31.551 --> 00:07:33.075
it travels through subspace,

00:07:33.152 --> 00:07:37.179
emerging occasionally
without warning.

00:07:37.256 --> 00:07:41.920
Ellipses have only been
observed a handful of times.

00:07:42.028 --> 00:07:43.188
Ares IV.

00:07:43.262 --> 00:07:44.729
Commander?

00:07:44.797 --> 00:07:47.732
One of the early Mars missions.

00:07:47.800 --> 00:07:50.496
The command module and
its pilot were engulfed

00:07:50.570 --> 00:07:53.664
by a similar phenomenon
back in 2032.

00:07:53.739 --> 00:07:55.263
I remember reading about that.

00:07:55.341 --> 00:07:58.071
Two astronauts were stranded
on the surface for weeks

00:07:58.144 --> 00:07:59.634
before a rescue ship arrived.

00:07:59.712 --> 00:08:02.112
No one's gotten this close
to a graviton ellipse

00:08:02.181 --> 00:08:03.512
and lived to tell about it.

00:08:03.583 --> 00:08:05.517
This could be a
remarkable opportunity.

00:08:05.585 --> 00:08:06.847
Go to Yellow Alert.

00:08:06.919 --> 00:08:08.887
Keep our power output
at minimum levels.

00:08:09.021 --> 00:08:11.717
Match its course and speed,
but maintain a safe distance.

00:08:11.791 --> 00:08:13.554
Aye, Captain.

00:08:13.626 --> 00:08:14.888
I suggest we launch a probe.

00:08:14.994 --> 00:08:17.519
See if we can find out
what makes this thing tick.

00:08:17.597 --> 00:08:18.621
Well, make it fast.

00:08:18.698 --> 00:08:20.461
There's no telling
when our visitor

00:08:20.533 --> 00:08:22.524
is going to burrow back
into subspace.

00:08:31.744 --> 00:08:34.577
I'm receiving telemetry
from the probe.

00:08:38.684 --> 00:08:40.845
It appears to have
entered a stable core

00:08:40.920 --> 00:08:42.148
within the anomaly,

00:08:42.221 --> 00:08:44.451
where gravimetric forces
are negligible.

00:08:44.524 --> 00:08:45.752
The eye of the storm.

00:08:45.825 --> 00:08:47.486
An apt metaphor.

00:08:47.560 --> 00:08:50.154
Computer, run a
multispectral analysis

00:08:50.229 --> 00:08:51.753
of the anomaly's core.

00:08:51.831 --> 00:08:53.924
Analysis in progress.

00:08:54.033 --> 00:08:55.660
The Borg developed shields

00:08:55.735 --> 00:08:57.930
to get through
the gravimetric currents.

00:08:58.037 --> 00:09:00.767
They intended to dissipate
the anomaly from within.

00:09:00.840 --> 00:09:03.138
Perhaps we should
continue their efforts.

00:09:03.209 --> 00:09:05.268
It would be shortsighted
to destroy it.

00:09:05.344 --> 00:09:07.312
We should study the phenomenon.

00:09:10.182 --> 00:09:11.479
I didn't realize

00:09:11.551 --> 00:09:14.315
you shared this crew's
penchant for exploration.

00:09:14.387 --> 00:09:16.355
I am a Starfleet officer.

00:09:16.422 --> 00:09:18.856
When the risks outweigh
the potential gain,

00:09:18.925 --> 00:09:20.654
exploration is illogical.

00:09:20.726 --> 00:09:24.560
We can't predict
what we might find here, Seven.

00:09:24.630 --> 00:09:28.031
One must allow
for the unexpected discovery.

00:09:28.100 --> 00:09:30.500
Core analysis complete.

00:09:32.905 --> 00:09:36.807
There are more than 2.8 billion
compounds in the core.

00:09:36.876 --> 00:09:39.037
Fascinating.

00:09:39.111 --> 00:09:40.476
The computer has isolated

00:09:40.546 --> 00:09:45.108
several synthetic alloys native
to Sector 001.

00:09:45.184 --> 00:09:47.015
Titanium
and polymer composites.

00:09:47.086 --> 00:09:49.748
These materials date back
to the 21st century.

00:09:49.822 --> 00:09:51.813
They were used
in the construction

00:09:51.891 --> 00:09:53.449
of Earth's early spacecraft.

00:09:53.526 --> 00:09:55.926
Commander Chakotay
mentioned a space capsule

00:09:56.028 --> 00:09:58.292
that was consumed
by a graviton ellipse.

00:09:58.364 --> 00:10:02.095
These alloys are consistent
with the hull of that capsule.

00:10:05.638 --> 00:10:08.869
An unexpected discovery, indeed.

00:10:20.753 --> 00:10:22.846
She was 46 meters,
92 metric tons,

00:10:22.922 --> 00:10:25.413
powered by a third-generation
ion drive,

00:10:25.491 --> 00:10:27.959
and equipped
with a transpectral imager.

00:10:28.094 --> 00:10:31.222
Ares IVwas piloted
by Lieutenant John Kelly.

00:10:31.297 --> 00:10:33.857
His ground team,
astronauts Rose Kumagawa

00:10:33.933 --> 00:10:35.332
and Andrei Novakovich,

00:10:35.401 --> 00:10:38.234
were close to completing
their survey mission

00:10:38.304 --> 00:10:41.762
when Kelly reported an object
closing on his position.

00:10:41.841 --> 00:10:43.308
Then he and the command module

00:10:43.376 --> 00:10:45.344
disappeared
off NASA's LIDAR scopes.

00:10:45.411 --> 00:10:46.673
And into the history books.

00:10:46.746 --> 00:10:49.146
That incident almost derailed
the Mars program.

00:10:49.215 --> 00:10:52.981
Mankind's first encounter
with a spatial anomaly.

00:10:53.119 --> 00:10:54.814
Seven, you said
the Borg were working

00:10:54.887 --> 00:10:56.855
on a way to get inside
a graviton ellipse.

00:10:56.922 --> 00:10:59.584
Shield enhancements.

00:10:59.659 --> 00:11:00.956
Well, maybe we could apply

00:11:01.027 --> 00:11:02.688
the same enhancements
to the Flyer.

00:11:02.762 --> 00:11:05.060
Captain, do you intend to try
to find the command module?

00:11:05.131 --> 00:11:07.031
Or what's left of it.

00:11:07.099 --> 00:11:09.761
By my calculations,
we have less than 16 hours

00:11:09.835 --> 00:11:12.360
before the anomaly
goes back into subspace.

00:11:12.438 --> 00:11:13.928
Then we have to work quickly.

00:11:14.073 --> 00:11:16.735
Harry, B'Elanna, help Seven
modify the Flyer's shields.

00:11:16.809 --> 00:11:19.937
Tom, review the database
from the Ares IVmission.

00:11:20.079 --> 00:11:22.206
It might tell us something
about this anomaly

00:11:22.281 --> 00:11:23.339
we've missed.

00:11:23.416 --> 00:11:26.544
We're going to need
a mission leader.

00:11:26.619 --> 00:11:28.211
I volunteer.

00:11:28.287 --> 00:11:29.345
I thought you might.

00:11:29.422 --> 00:11:31.549
Let's do it.

00:11:35.928 --> 00:11:37.555
Something on your mind,
Seven?

00:11:37.630 --> 00:11:40.064
The anomaly is as dangerous
and unpredictable now

00:11:40.132 --> 00:11:42.623
as it was 300 years ago.

00:11:42.702 --> 00:11:44.602
Well, the Delta Flyer is
a little more advanced

00:11:44.670 --> 00:11:45.602
than the Ares IVwas.

00:11:45.671 --> 00:11:46.968
We'll be taking
every precaution.

00:11:47.106 --> 00:11:49.438
That may not be enough.

00:11:49.508 --> 00:11:52.966
I appreciate your concern,
but this is my call.

00:11:53.045 --> 00:11:55.878
Searching
for the command module seems

00:11:55.948 --> 00:11:57.745
more sentimental
than scientific.

00:11:57.817 --> 00:12:00.684
Well, I can't argue with that.

00:12:00.753 --> 00:12:03.187
If scientific knowledge
was all we were after,

00:12:03.255 --> 00:12:05.189
then the Federation
would have built

00:12:05.257 --> 00:12:07.020
a fleet of probes,
not starships.

00:12:07.093 --> 00:12:09.926
Exploration is about seeing
things with your own eyes.

00:12:10.062 --> 00:12:12.155
In this case,
we're exploring the past.

00:12:12.231 --> 00:12:14.699
How will retrieving
this artifact

00:12:14.767 --> 00:12:16.564
enhance your appreciation
of history?

00:12:16.635 --> 00:12:19.331
By making us part of it,
in the same way

00:12:19.405 --> 00:12:22.568
that excavating the obelisks
of ancient Vulcan,

00:12:22.641 --> 00:12:24.700
or finding
the Shroud of Kahless

00:12:24.777 --> 00:12:28.235
made those explorers
part of their history.

00:12:29.582 --> 00:12:31.447
Here's the crux of it.

00:12:33.419 --> 00:12:37.515
As a Borg, you didn't study
the past; you ingested it.

00:12:37.590 --> 00:12:39.319
You never really developed

00:12:39.391 --> 00:12:41.689
an appreciation for
humanity's history.

00:12:41.761 --> 00:12:43.228
Maybe this is an opportunity

00:12:43.295 --> 00:12:45.695
for you to do some
exploring of your own.

00:12:45.765 --> 00:12:48.427
Are you ordering me
to join this mission?

00:12:48.501 --> 00:12:51.766
Let's just say I'm encouraging
you to volunteer.

00:12:56.542 --> 00:12:58.510
It's no solar flare.

00:13:00.346 --> 00:13:02.041
Wow.

00:13:02.114 --> 00:13:04.344
John, can you describe it?

00:13:04.416 --> 00:13:07.715
Lt... it's at least
1,000 meters wide.

00:13:07.787 --> 00:13:08.913
Bright.

00:13:08.988 --> 00:13:10.717
Your transmission's breaking up.

00:13:10.790 --> 00:13:13.258
It's generating
an electromagnetic radiation

00:13:13.325 --> 00:13:15.156
interfering
with primary systems.

00:13:15.227 --> 00:13:17.525
I can't get away from it.

00:13:17.596 --> 00:13:20.292
Activating
the transpectral imager.

00:13:20.366 --> 00:13:23.062
I'll record
as much data as I can.

00:13:24.770 --> 00:13:26.169
It's right on top of me!

00:13:26.238 --> 00:13:28.001
I'll transmit as much as I ca...

00:13:29.341 --> 00:13:31.241
That's all she wrote.

00:13:33.012 --> 00:13:37.108
NASA received Kelly's
last telemetry

00:13:37.183 --> 00:13:40.812
at 0922 hours, October 19, 2032.

00:13:40.886 --> 00:13:43.184
I thought I was the Mars buff.

00:13:43.255 --> 00:13:44.950
You seem to know more
about the Ares IV

00:13:45.024 --> 00:13:46.286
than I do.

00:13:46.358 --> 00:13:49.623
The Mars missions paved the way
for the exploration of space.

00:13:49.695 --> 00:13:51.663
Kelly was one
of my childhood heroes.

00:13:51.730 --> 00:13:54.563
Yeah, mine, too.

00:13:54.633 --> 00:13:55.565
That's dedication.

00:13:55.634 --> 00:13:57.101
The man's life's about to end,

00:13:57.169 --> 00:13:58.966
but he won't stop
taking readings.

00:13:59.038 --> 00:14:00.801
Makes you wonder
if those old-timers

00:14:00.873 --> 00:14:03.137
were made of sterner
stuff than we are.

00:14:03.209 --> 00:14:04.733
You think we have it easy?

00:14:04.810 --> 00:14:05.742
Are you kidding?

00:14:05.811 --> 00:14:08.041
Warp drive, shields,
transporters...

00:14:08.113 --> 00:14:10.547
we're traveling
in the lap of luxury.

00:14:10.616 --> 00:14:13.346
Kelly and Kumagawa,
Armstrong and Glenn...

00:14:13.419 --> 00:14:15.353
they were the real pioneers.

00:14:15.421 --> 00:14:17.013
Am I interrupting?

00:14:17.089 --> 00:14:21.287
We're just admiring someone,
a fellow explorer.

00:14:21.360 --> 00:14:23.157
Hero worship...
the glorification

00:14:23.229 --> 00:14:25.220
of an individual's
accomplishments.

00:14:25.297 --> 00:14:27.765
I guess you didn't have
many role models growing up.

00:14:27.833 --> 00:14:28.925
In the collective,

00:14:29.001 --> 00:14:30.593
personal accomplishments
are irrelevant.

00:14:30.669 --> 00:14:33.365
The shield modifications
to the Delta Flyer are complete.

00:14:33.439 --> 00:14:34.531
We're ready for launch.

00:14:34.607 --> 00:14:35.539
We?

00:14:35.608 --> 00:14:37.542
You'll need someone familiar

00:14:37.610 --> 00:14:40.738
with Borg technology
to monitor the shields.

00:14:40.813 --> 00:14:41.939
I'm volunteering.

00:14:42.081 --> 00:14:44.447
Well, you don't sound
too happy about it.

00:14:44.516 --> 00:14:47.917
I'm not, but this mission
can benefit from my expertise.

00:14:47.987 --> 00:14:49.955
In that case...

00:14:50.022 --> 00:14:52.354
welcome aboard.

00:14:52.424 --> 00:14:53.948
I'm preparing an inoculant

00:14:54.093 --> 00:14:56.857
to counter the effects
of gravimetric radiation.

00:14:56.929 --> 00:14:58.521
Of course,
if you were a hologram,

00:14:58.597 --> 00:15:00.622
you'd simply adjust
your mobile emitter.

00:15:00.699 --> 00:15:02.189
That's what I did
on Arakis Prime,

00:15:02.268 --> 00:15:03.792
one of my first away missions.

00:15:03.869 --> 00:15:05.200
I don't recall that.

00:15:05.271 --> 00:15:06.829
This was before you came aboard.

00:15:06.906 --> 00:15:09.170
Such an enchanting planet...

00:15:09.241 --> 00:15:10.902
crystalline glaciers...

00:15:10.976 --> 00:15:12.876
magnesium vapor atmosphere.

00:15:12.945 --> 00:15:14.913
I had to stop my
metallurgical scans

00:15:14.980 --> 00:15:17.210
just to admire
the sheer beauty of it...

00:15:17.283 --> 00:15:20.912
to..."smell the roses,"
to coin a phrase.

00:15:21.020 --> 00:15:22.749
An inefficient use of your time.

00:15:22.821 --> 00:15:24.812
Hmm. Perhaps.

00:15:24.890 --> 00:15:26.653
We're all finished here,

00:15:26.725 --> 00:15:28.283
except for one thing.

00:15:29.929 --> 00:15:32.124
Would you mind
recording some images

00:15:32.197 --> 00:15:34.358
when you're inside the ellipse,

00:15:34.433 --> 00:15:36.924
for those of us not lucky
enough to make the trip?

00:15:37.002 --> 00:15:40.267
You envy my participation
on this mission.

00:15:40.339 --> 00:15:42.466
There isn't a crewman
aboard who doesn't.

00:15:42.541 --> 00:15:43.803
Why?

00:15:43.876 --> 00:15:46.572
I can only tell you
how I felt that morning

00:15:46.645 --> 00:15:48.909
when I materialized
on Arakis Prime.

00:15:48.981 --> 00:15:52.314
I left my footprints
in the magnesite dust,

00:15:52.384 --> 00:15:55.945
and thought, "One small
step for a hologram,

00:15:56.021 --> 00:15:58.353
one giant leap for mankind."

00:15:58.424 --> 00:16:00.449
To coin a phrase.

00:16:01.961 --> 00:16:05.192
Well, I know the sentiment
isn't exactly original,

00:16:05.264 --> 00:16:07.198
but the fact is
I was exhilarated.

00:16:07.266 --> 00:16:08.995
Chakotay to Seven of Nine.

00:16:09.068 --> 00:16:11.036
Report to Shuttle Bay 2.

00:16:11.103 --> 00:16:12.297
Good luck.

00:16:25.384 --> 00:16:27.011
We're approaching the perimeter

00:16:27.086 --> 00:16:30.419
in five, four...

00:16:30.489 --> 00:16:32.116
three, two,

00:16:32.191 --> 00:16:34.284
one.

00:16:36.595 --> 00:16:37.926
We're within 2,000 kilometers.

00:16:37.997 --> 00:16:39.760
Gravimetric interference
is increasing.

00:16:39.832 --> 00:16:42.232
It's nothing compared
to what they're going through.

00:16:42.301 --> 00:16:44.292
They're really getting
knocked around in there.

00:16:44.370 --> 00:16:47.032
The shields have weakened,
but they're still holding.

00:16:47.106 --> 00:16:48.596
Harry, keep your finger
on that tractor beam

00:16:48.674 --> 00:16:49.936
in case we have
to pull them out.

00:16:50.075 --> 00:16:52.737
They're approaching
the anomaly's core.

00:16:52.811 --> 00:16:54.335
We've lost sensor contact.

00:16:54.413 --> 00:16:55.505
Harry.

00:16:55.581 --> 00:16:57.310
The com link is still active.

00:17:00.919 --> 00:17:03.149
The gravimetric shear
is increasing.

00:17:03.222 --> 00:17:05.520
I'm reading diminished
turbulence directly ahead.

00:17:05.591 --> 00:17:07.058
Full thrusters, Tom.

00:17:07.126 --> 00:17:08.423
Punch us through.

00:17:15.467 --> 00:17:17.367
Chakotay to Voyager.

00:17:19.204 --> 00:17:21.263
We're in.

00:17:26.812 --> 00:17:28.609
I wish you could see this,
Captain.

00:17:28.680 --> 00:17:29.612
It's incredible.

00:17:29.681 --> 00:17:30.773
We're all ears.

00:17:30.849 --> 00:17:33.215
It's very calm,
no gravimetric distortions.

00:17:33.285 --> 00:17:36.914
The EM activity is creating
a natural luminescence.

00:17:36.989 --> 00:17:38.854
Tom called it "mood lighting."

00:17:38.924 --> 00:17:41.415
Hey, B'Elanna, it's
the perfect romantic getaway.

00:17:41.493 --> 00:17:43.085
I'll take your word for it.

00:17:43.162 --> 00:17:46.495
We've detected asteroid
fragments, pieces of vessels,

00:17:46.565 --> 00:17:48.465
matter from every Quadrant
of the galaxy.

00:17:48.534 --> 00:17:50.764
Next time I lose something,
I'll know where to look.

00:17:50.836 --> 00:17:52.133
Instead of a graviton ellipse,

00:17:52.204 --> 00:17:54.729
we should call it
the "Kitchen Sink Anomaly."

00:17:54.807 --> 00:17:56.468
The chemical interactions
have even created

00:17:56.542 --> 00:17:58.134
a primitive atmosphere.

00:17:58.210 --> 00:18:00.405
Nothing you'd want
to breathe, of course.

00:18:00.479 --> 00:18:02.811
What do you make of it, Seven?

00:18:02.881 --> 00:18:06.373
Well, I suppose it's intriguing.

00:18:06.452 --> 00:18:09.888
Some of the matter appears to be
extra-dimensional in origin.

00:18:09.988 --> 00:18:13.287
Sounds like this anomaly's
gotten around.

00:18:13.358 --> 00:18:15.588
Any sign
of the Ares command module?

00:18:15.661 --> 00:18:17.595
We're picking up traces
of the hull,

00:18:17.663 --> 00:18:19.893
but our sensor readings
are being refracted

00:18:19.965 --> 00:18:21.330
by all the debris in here.

00:18:23.068 --> 00:18:25.059
That was a gravimetric surge,

00:18:25.137 --> 00:18:28.038
caused by the anomaly
altering its course

00:18:28.107 --> 00:18:29.369
by.006 degrees.

00:18:29.441 --> 00:18:31.432
We just had a big jolt
out here.

00:18:31.510 --> 00:18:33.137
We didn't feel a thing.

00:18:33.212 --> 00:18:35.146
By our estimate, you have
five hours, 36 minutes

00:18:35.214 --> 00:18:36.875
before the anomaly
returns to subspace.

00:18:36.949 --> 00:18:38.246
You better get a move on.

00:18:38.317 --> 00:18:40.114
Understood.

00:18:40.185 --> 00:18:41.914
I'm having difficulty

00:18:41.987 --> 00:18:44.182
isolating the debris
from the command module.

00:18:44.256 --> 00:18:46.588
I'll lay in a search pattern.

00:18:46.658 --> 00:18:49.559
It may take a couple of hours
to cover an area this large.

00:18:49.628 --> 00:18:52.096
Seven and I can use the time
to take some samples.

00:18:52.164 --> 00:18:55.099
Get a better idea
of where this anomaly's been.

00:18:55.167 --> 00:18:57.362
We should focus on
achieving our objective

00:18:57.436 --> 00:18:58.733
and returning to Voyager.

00:18:58.804 --> 00:19:00.533
We're not here
just to find the module.

00:19:00.606 --> 00:19:01.937
We've got an opportunity to...

00:19:02.007 --> 00:19:02.871
To explore.

00:19:03.008 --> 00:19:05.238
I am familiar with our mission.

00:19:05.310 --> 00:19:08.438
In that case, let's get started.

00:19:17.956 --> 00:19:19.890
The fossilized
microbes in this ore

00:19:20.025 --> 00:19:22.152
appear to have had
metallic membranes.

00:19:22.227 --> 00:19:25.424
We've speculated
about the possibility

00:19:25.497 --> 00:19:30.196
of metallic life-forms,
but we've never discovered one.

00:19:31.270 --> 00:19:33.101
You're excited
by this discovery.

00:19:34.806 --> 00:19:36.000
This piece of rock

00:19:36.074 --> 00:19:38.201
is billions of years
older than Earth...

00:19:38.277 --> 00:19:41.144
from a time when the
galaxy was still forming.

00:19:41.213 --> 00:19:42.202
Here.

00:19:42.281 --> 00:19:43.748
Commander.

00:19:43.815 --> 00:19:45.009
Take it.

00:19:48.353 --> 00:19:50.685
You're holding
a piece of history...

00:19:50.756 --> 00:19:52.883
maybe even the beginnings
of life itself.

00:19:52.958 --> 00:19:54.482
Unlikely.

00:19:54.560 --> 00:19:56.528
It's more likely than you think.

00:19:56.595 --> 00:19:57.892
This anomaly is as old

00:19:57.963 --> 00:19:59.931
as anything
we've ever encountered.

00:20:00.065 --> 00:20:01.498
I could spend a lifetime

00:20:01.567 --> 00:20:03.626
studying the things
it's collected.

00:20:03.702 --> 00:20:06.364
And leave Voyager
without a First Officer?

00:20:06.438 --> 00:20:08.702
They'd manage.

00:20:08.774 --> 00:20:11.436
Paleontology was
always my first love.

00:20:11.510 --> 00:20:13.944
It's the reason
I joined Starfleet.

00:20:14.079 --> 00:20:15.478
Why didn't you pursue it?

00:20:15.547 --> 00:20:18.448
My sense of responsibility
got in the way...

00:20:18.517 --> 00:20:21.543
first the Maquis, then Voyager.

00:20:21.620 --> 00:20:24.783
What I wanted always seemed
to take a backseat.

00:20:24.856 --> 00:20:27.552
Do you remember
what you wanted to be

00:20:27.626 --> 00:20:29.753
before you were assimilated?

00:20:29.828 --> 00:20:33.389
I was assimilated
when I was a child.

00:20:33.465 --> 00:20:37.765
I knew I wanted to be a
paleontologist when I was six.

00:20:41.139 --> 00:20:43.232
A ballerina.

00:20:45.577 --> 00:20:47.169
Maybe it's not too late.

00:20:47.246 --> 00:20:49.737
It was a juvenile fantasy.

00:20:49.815 --> 00:20:51.806
Those are the ones
that stick with you.

00:20:51.883 --> 00:20:53.282
Heads up, folks.

00:20:53.352 --> 00:20:55.252
I think we're getting close.

00:20:58.457 --> 00:21:00.721
I've isolated the module.

00:21:00.792 --> 00:21:02.623
Bearing 39 mark 15.

00:21:02.694 --> 00:21:04.685
There.

00:21:09.268 --> 00:21:11.133
It appears to be intact.

00:21:11.203 --> 00:21:13.467
I'm reading hull
breaches and corrosion,

00:21:13.538 --> 00:21:16.098
but all things considered,
it's pretty well preserved.

00:21:16.174 --> 00:21:18.404
We weren't expecting
to find a fragment this big.

00:21:18.477 --> 00:21:20.945
We can't just stow it
in the aft section.

00:21:22.180 --> 00:21:25.445
We'll use a tractor beam
to tow it back to Voyager.

00:21:26.518 --> 00:21:27.610
Captain, the anomaly just made

00:21:27.686 --> 00:21:30.280
another course change...
.003 degrees.

00:21:30.355 --> 00:21:31.947
That's the third one in an hour.

00:21:32.024 --> 00:21:33.719
Something has to be
attracting it.

00:21:33.792 --> 00:21:35.555
There's nothing on the sensors.

00:21:35.627 --> 00:21:37.652
I think it enjoys
being unpredictable.

00:21:37.729 --> 00:21:40.493
We know it has a taste
for electromagnetic energy.

00:21:40.565 --> 00:21:43.056
So what could generate
an EM field large enough

00:21:43.135 --> 00:21:45.968
to get its attention but
still not show up on sensors?

00:21:46.038 --> 00:21:49.701
An energy burst from a pulsar,
a neutrino cloud.

00:21:49.775 --> 00:21:50.969
Dark matter.

00:21:51.043 --> 00:21:54.638
Realign the sensor array
to scan for EM fluctuations.

00:21:55.847 --> 00:21:58.179
I've got
a spatial disturbance...

00:21:58.250 --> 00:22:00.115
three million kilometers away.

00:22:00.185 --> 00:22:01.846
It's a dark-matter asteroid.

00:22:01.920 --> 00:22:02.909
Bingo.

00:22:02.988 --> 00:22:04.683
The anomaly's heading
right for it.

00:22:04.756 --> 00:22:05.848
Time to impact?

00:22:05.924 --> 00:22:07.824
Four minutes.
Captain,

00:22:07.893 --> 00:22:10.327
we can't predict how the impact
will affect the anomaly.

00:22:10.395 --> 00:22:13.262
But it's a safe bet
it won't be a gentle nudge.

00:22:13.332 --> 00:22:14.924
Janeway to Delta Flyer.

00:22:15.033 --> 00:22:16.125
We've got a problem.

00:22:16.201 --> 00:22:17.634
The anomaly's
on a collision course

00:22:17.703 --> 00:22:18.863
with a dark-matter asteroid.

00:22:18.937 --> 00:22:20.165
We've located the Mars orbiter.

00:22:20.238 --> 00:22:22.172
We're adapting a tractor beam.

00:22:22.240 --> 00:22:23.172
Give us a minute.

00:22:23.241 --> 00:22:24.333
You don't have it;
get out of there.

00:22:24.409 --> 00:22:25.137
Captain...

00:22:25.210 --> 00:22:25.938
Now.

00:22:26.044 --> 00:22:29.172
Is the tractor beam ready?

00:22:29.247 --> 00:22:30.373
Just about, sir.

00:22:30.449 --> 00:22:33.179
Lock onto the module
and take us out.

00:22:33.251 --> 00:22:34.616
We don't have time.

00:22:34.686 --> 00:22:36.119
I gave you an order.

00:22:36.188 --> 00:22:37.450
Commander.

00:22:37.522 --> 00:22:39.922
We're not leaving
without that module.

00:22:41.793 --> 00:22:42.953
Tom!

00:22:50.402 --> 00:22:51.699
Captain, both the anomaly

00:22:51.770 --> 00:22:53.237
and the asteroid
are accelerating.

00:22:53.305 --> 00:22:55.671
They're attracting each other
like a pair of magnets.

00:22:55.741 --> 00:22:57.732
At this rate, it'll impact in...

00:22:57.809 --> 00:22:58.798
56 seconds.

00:22:58.877 --> 00:23:00.674
Chakotay, you've got
less than a minute.

00:23:00.746 --> 00:23:01.735
Acknowledged.

00:23:01.813 --> 00:23:04.247
The module is slowing us down.

00:23:04.316 --> 00:23:05.544
Maintain tractor lock.

00:23:05.617 --> 00:23:07.676
How long until we clear
the anomaly?

00:23:07.753 --> 00:23:09.914
At our current speed,
40 seconds.

00:23:09.988 --> 00:23:10.977
We can do it.

00:23:14.426 --> 00:23:17.418
30 seconds to impact.
They're still not clear.

00:23:17.496 --> 00:23:18.895
Chakotay, status.

00:23:18.964 --> 00:23:21.194
We're on our way.

00:23:23.135 --> 00:23:24.500
20 seconds.

00:23:42.954 --> 00:23:45.252
Gravimetric distortions
are increasing.

00:23:45.323 --> 00:23:46.255
The Flyer.

00:23:46.324 --> 00:23:47.723
I've lost the signal.

00:24:01.740 --> 00:24:03.469
How badly is he damaged?

00:24:03.542 --> 00:24:06.033
He's got a severe concussion
and internal injuries.

00:24:06.111 --> 00:24:07.601
We need to get him
back to Voyager.

00:24:07.679 --> 00:24:09.010
That may not be possible.

00:24:09.080 --> 00:24:10.741
Communications,
shields and propulsion

00:24:10.816 --> 00:24:12.044
are still off-line.

00:24:12.117 --> 00:24:13.414
Well, he's stable for now.

00:24:13.485 --> 00:24:14.782
I'll give you a hand.

00:24:14.853 --> 00:24:16.115
We can repair the shields,

00:24:16.188 --> 00:24:18.213
but the engines
are unsalvageable.

00:24:18.290 --> 00:24:19.587
It can't be that bad.

00:24:19.658 --> 00:24:20.818
It's worse.

00:24:20.892 --> 00:24:23.918
The anomaly is showing signs
of returning to subspace.

00:24:24.062 --> 00:24:25.723
We have less than two hours.

00:24:43.815 --> 00:24:46.249
He's regaining consciousness.

00:24:46.318 --> 00:24:47.512
Easy.

00:24:47.586 --> 00:24:49.349
Easy. Lie still.

00:24:49.421 --> 00:24:52.117
It feels like I went ten rounds
with an Andorian.

00:24:52.190 --> 00:24:53.384
More like 20.

00:24:53.458 --> 00:24:55.926
You were hit
with a plasma discharge.

00:24:55.994 --> 00:24:58.622
The gravimetric surge
from the asteroid

00:24:58.697 --> 00:25:02.531
caught us at the edge of the
anomaly, tossed us back inside.

00:25:02.601 --> 00:25:04.364
The command module?

00:25:04.436 --> 00:25:06.631
It's adrift, 300 meters
off our port bow.

00:25:06.705 --> 00:25:08.229
Then we can still salvage it.

00:25:08.306 --> 00:25:10.103
Not so fast.

00:25:10.175 --> 00:25:12.268
We can't get engines
or shields back on line,

00:25:12.344 --> 00:25:13.834
not to mention a tractor beam.

00:25:13.912 --> 00:25:15.277
We'll be lucky
to get out of this

00:25:15.347 --> 00:25:16.405
in one piece.

00:25:16.481 --> 00:25:17.413
I can help.

00:25:17.482 --> 00:25:19.450
You're in worse shape
than this ship.

00:25:19.518 --> 00:25:20.450
You want to help?

00:25:20.519 --> 00:25:21.577
Lie in the bed...
doctor's orders.

00:25:23.822 --> 00:25:25.585
Another gravimetric surge.

00:25:25.657 --> 00:25:29.787
I'll see if I can reinforce
the structural integrity field.

00:25:33.899 --> 00:25:36.891
You might try drawing power
from auxiliary life support.

00:25:36.968 --> 00:25:38.595
The system was
damaged as well.

00:25:38.670 --> 00:25:40.763
What about
the secondary relays?

00:25:40.839 --> 00:25:42.067
They're off-line.

00:25:42.140 --> 00:25:43.937
That doesn't leave us
many choices.

00:25:44.042 --> 00:25:46.067
Try rerouting
the phaser couplings.

00:25:46.144 --> 00:25:49.272
We explored that option
while you were unconscious.

00:25:49.347 --> 00:25:50.871
Then maybe you can
bring me up-to-date.

00:25:50.949 --> 00:25:51.881
There's no time.

00:25:51.950 --> 00:25:53.281
I suggest you recuperate.

00:25:53.351 --> 00:25:55.285
We have the situation
under control.

00:25:55.353 --> 00:25:57.150
It doesn't look that way
from here.

00:25:57.222 --> 00:25:59.190
I'm still in command
of this mission.

00:25:59.257 --> 00:26:00.918
I want to know what's going on.

00:26:00.992 --> 00:26:03.153
Very well.

00:26:03.228 --> 00:26:05.890
We were ordered by the Captain
to leave this anomaly,

00:26:06.031 --> 00:26:08.022
but you chose
to disobey that command.

00:26:08.099 --> 00:26:11.364
As a result of your obsession
with the module,

00:26:11.436 --> 00:26:13.097
we are now trapped
along with it.

00:26:13.171 --> 00:26:15.605
In all probability,
we won't survive without...

00:26:15.674 --> 00:26:16.800
Point taken.

00:26:16.875 --> 00:26:20.572
Now, why don't you continue
without the attitude?

00:26:23.114 --> 00:26:25.912
An inventory
of the damaged systems.

00:26:26.051 --> 00:26:27.814
Read it for yourself.

00:26:34.326 --> 00:26:35.623
I made a mistake.

00:26:35.694 --> 00:26:37.685
I put the mission at risk.

00:26:37.762 --> 00:26:39.855
You put our lives at risk.

00:26:43.735 --> 00:26:46.670
Well, if we don't make it out...

00:26:48.073 --> 00:26:50.337
someone's bound
to come looking for us

00:26:50.408 --> 00:26:51.841
in a few hundred years.

00:26:54.145 --> 00:26:58.582
Diffusing a tense situation
with humor... remember?

00:26:58.650 --> 00:27:01.175
Voyager to Delta Flyer.

00:27:03.521 --> 00:27:06.081
They're using a probe
as a transmission relay.

00:27:06.157 --> 00:27:08.148
Voyager to Chakotay,
please respond.

00:27:08.226 --> 00:27:09.659
We can't detect you.

00:27:09.728 --> 00:27:11.059
Captain, we can hear you.

00:27:11.129 --> 00:27:12.994
I'm matching their frequency.

00:27:13.064 --> 00:27:15.828
Voyager, this is
the Delta Flyer. Respond.

00:27:15.900 --> 00:27:17.561
Are you all right?

00:27:17.636 --> 00:27:18.864
What's your status?

00:27:18.937 --> 00:27:22.703
We've sustained heavy damage,
Captain, but we're alive.

00:27:22.774 --> 00:27:24.765
It's good to hear your voice.

00:27:27.245 --> 00:27:29.008
We could install
the same shielding

00:27:29.080 --> 00:27:31.071
on a class-2 shuttle,
and take it in.

00:27:31.149 --> 00:27:33.344
It took us hours
to make the modifications

00:27:33.418 --> 00:27:34.442
to the Flyer.

00:27:34.519 --> 00:27:35.781
We've only got 82 minutes

00:27:35.854 --> 00:27:38.084
before this thing disappears
into subspace.

00:27:38.156 --> 00:27:40.386
It may be possible
to modify a tractor beam

00:27:40.458 --> 00:27:42.790
to cut through the
gravimetric interference.

00:27:42.861 --> 00:27:45.796
You'll never be able to get
it all the way to the core.

00:27:45.864 --> 00:27:47.297
Start working on it anyway.

00:27:47.365 --> 00:27:49.629
You said all the energy conduits
are fused?

00:27:49.701 --> 00:27:50.759
Correct.

00:27:50.835 --> 00:27:52.928
We can't get power
to any of the primary systems.

00:27:53.038 --> 00:27:54.369
If the replicators were working,

00:27:54.439 --> 00:27:56.100
we could whip up
a new plasma manifold

00:27:56.174 --> 00:27:57.505
and be on our way.

00:27:57.575 --> 00:28:00.271
What if I could get you
an old manifold?

00:28:00.345 --> 00:28:01.607
Excuse me?

00:28:01.680 --> 00:28:04.513
The command module
may be three centuries old,

00:28:04.582 --> 00:28:06.641
but the power
distribution system

00:28:06.718 --> 00:28:09.118
isn't that different
from the Flyer's.

00:28:09.187 --> 00:28:10.654
Right here.

00:28:10.722 --> 00:28:13.316
This control panel
in the main cockpit...

00:28:13.391 --> 00:28:15.586
it was called
an ion distributor.

00:28:15.660 --> 00:28:19.152
With a few tweaks, it could be
modified to channel warp plasma.

00:28:19.230 --> 00:28:22.427
Even if it were possible,
we'd have to obtain the object

00:28:22.500 --> 00:28:23.467
before we can adapt it.

00:28:23.535 --> 00:28:24.467
Do you have

00:28:24.536 --> 00:28:26.436
enough power to beam one of you
to the module?

00:28:26.504 --> 00:28:28.062
Yes, ma'am. I'll go.

00:28:28.139 --> 00:28:30.130
Not so fast, Tom.

00:28:30.208 --> 00:28:32.039
If another
gravimetric surge hits,

00:28:32.110 --> 00:28:33.634
we'll need you at the helm.

00:28:33.712 --> 00:28:34.906
Seven?

00:28:35.046 --> 00:28:39.005
Are you encouraging me
to volunteer again, Captain?

00:28:39.084 --> 00:28:41.518
You read my mind.

00:28:41.586 --> 00:28:43.520
Come on.

00:28:43.588 --> 00:28:46.455
Just a little closer.

00:28:54.265 --> 00:28:55.664
That's it for thrusters,

00:28:55.734 --> 00:28:58.100
but I got us
within transporter range. Ready?

00:28:58.169 --> 00:28:59.864
Stand by.

00:29:11.816 --> 00:29:15.616
Ironic. You're doing
what I've always dreamt of.

00:29:15.687 --> 00:29:20.317
Remember, when you
set foot in that module,

00:29:20.391 --> 00:29:22.484
you'll be stepping into history.

00:29:22.560 --> 00:29:24.494
History is irrelevant.

00:29:24.562 --> 00:29:26.530
Irrelevant?

00:29:28.833 --> 00:29:30.596
Do me a favor.

00:29:30.668 --> 00:29:32.465
When you're over there,

00:29:32.537 --> 00:29:36.268
download whatever you can
from their database

00:29:36.341 --> 00:29:39.401
and also, take a minute
to look around...

00:29:39.477 --> 00:29:42.412
so you can tell us
what it was like.

00:29:42.480 --> 00:29:44.038
There may not be time...

00:29:48.019 --> 00:29:49.884
but I'll try.

00:29:49.954 --> 00:29:52.923
Good luck.

00:30:05.470 --> 00:30:07.404
What's it like in there?

00:30:07.472 --> 00:30:08.803
Dark.

00:30:13.845 --> 00:30:17.440
The ambient temperature
is minus 260 degrees.

00:30:19.818 --> 00:30:23.151
There appear to be fractures
in the aft bulkheads.

00:30:23.221 --> 00:30:24.745
Is the cockpit intact?

00:30:24.823 --> 00:30:26.518
Yes.

00:30:47.212 --> 00:30:50.306
I've brought the main computer
on line.

00:30:50.381 --> 00:30:54.442
I've lost contact
with the team on the surface,

00:30:54.519 --> 00:30:57.420
and I can't get a fix
on my position.

00:30:57.488 --> 00:30:58.978
What was that?

00:30:59.123 --> 00:31:00.647
There's an active data file.

00:31:00.725 --> 00:31:02.590
It appears
to contain log entries.

00:31:02.660 --> 00:31:04.560
Can you play them for us?

00:31:05.997 --> 00:31:07.828
I believe so.

00:31:10.501 --> 00:31:13.095
Consider it my last request.

00:31:19.444 --> 00:31:22.607
...and I can't get
a fix on my position,

00:31:22.680 --> 00:31:27.379
but I'm alive, and the CM seems
to be intact.

00:31:29.087 --> 00:31:31.715
I'm inside this whatever it is.

00:31:31.789 --> 00:31:33.381
Could you hear that, Chakotay?

00:31:33.458 --> 00:31:35.688
It's amazing.

00:31:35.760 --> 00:31:38.820
Everyone assumed
he was killed instantly.

00:31:38.897 --> 00:31:40.592
...what I've gotten myself into.

00:31:40.665 --> 00:31:42.963
It's very calm, like I'm
in the eye of a hurricane.

00:31:43.034 --> 00:31:44.626
It's a little spooky.

00:31:44.702 --> 00:31:46.260
The way I figure it,

00:31:46.337 --> 00:31:48.464
if there's a way in,
there's got to be a way out.

00:31:48.539 --> 00:31:50.507
I'm going to fire up
the transpectral imager

00:31:50.575 --> 00:31:52.668
before I initiate
another engine burn...

00:31:53.978 --> 00:31:56.742
...collect as much data
as I can.

00:31:56.814 --> 00:31:59.806
Jeannie, I may not be bringing
you back a Mars rock,

00:31:59.884 --> 00:32:01.784
but I promise you,

00:32:01.853 --> 00:32:03.753
I'm going to do everything
I can to get home.

00:32:08.826 --> 00:32:12.023
Ares IV Mission Record,
October 23.

00:32:12.096 --> 00:32:14.360
I'm starting to feel like Jonah.

00:32:14.432 --> 00:32:17.367
How long was he in the belly
of that whale? Three days?

00:32:17.435 --> 00:32:21.303
I got him beat
by a day or two already.

00:32:24.943 --> 00:32:26.501
The EM interference

00:32:26.577 --> 00:32:28.875
is disrupting communications
and LIDAR,

00:32:28.947 --> 00:32:30.710
and I can't see the stars,

00:32:30.782 --> 00:32:33.751
so there's no way
I can get a fix on my position.

00:32:33.818 --> 00:32:36.286
If this thing's moving as fast
as it was when it pulled me in,

00:32:36.354 --> 00:32:38.754
I could be
a long way from Mars by now.

00:32:40.258 --> 00:32:42.089
The imager's
been working overtime

00:32:42.160 --> 00:32:43.889
cataloguing
all the matter in here,

00:32:43.962 --> 00:32:46.430
but a lot of it defies analysis.

00:32:46.497 --> 00:32:47.555
I think we're going to need

00:32:47.632 --> 00:32:49.099
to make room
on the periodic table.

00:32:53.404 --> 00:32:56.498
Where the hell am I?

00:32:56.574 --> 00:32:58.974
Power levels have dropped
another 15 percent

00:32:59.077 --> 00:33:01.011
since my last systems check.

00:33:28.339 --> 00:33:32.867
I... I think I just saw
another spacecraft.

00:33:32.944 --> 00:33:34.707
Either that...

00:33:34.779 --> 00:33:37.748
or all this dehydrated food
has gone to my head.

00:33:43.287 --> 00:33:44.720
I got it.

00:33:44.789 --> 00:33:47.087
The hull's made
of some kind of alloy.

00:33:47.158 --> 00:33:49.217
I can't make heads
or tails of it.

00:33:49.293 --> 00:33:51.488
I shouldn't have been
so dismissive.

00:33:51.562 --> 00:33:54.190
I remember
giving Kumagawa a hard time

00:33:54.265 --> 00:33:57.428
when she claimed
she saw a UFO over the Gulf.

00:33:57.502 --> 00:33:59.902
I told her it was a meteor

00:33:59.971 --> 00:34:02.462
or another piece of Muir.

00:34:02.540 --> 00:34:05.805
I guess I owe you
an apology, Rose.

00:34:11.049 --> 00:34:17.147
Ares IV Mission Record,
October 25.

00:34:17.221 --> 00:34:19.246
This has been
a nice place to visit,

00:34:19.323 --> 00:34:20.915
but I'm ready to come home.

00:34:21.025 --> 00:34:22.652
I've prepped the ion drive,

00:34:22.727 --> 00:34:25.321
channeled all the power
from the thruster reserves

00:34:25.396 --> 00:34:26.761
into the main tank.

00:34:26.831 --> 00:34:31.165
I have enough fuel
for one last engine burn.

00:34:31.235 --> 00:34:33.203
Wish me luck.

00:34:47.418 --> 00:34:48.942
Ignition sequence.

00:34:50.855 --> 00:34:54.484
Five, four, three, two...

00:34:56.661 --> 00:35:00.290
I'm losing pitch control.

00:35:00.364 --> 00:35:02.127
Gyros aren't responding.

00:35:03.701 --> 00:35:04.861
Power failure.

00:35:04.936 --> 00:35:06.563
I've got to abort.

00:35:06.637 --> 00:35:08.628
I'm not going to make it!

00:35:19.784 --> 00:35:23.311
All systems go.

00:35:23.387 --> 00:35:24.820
Watch me, Dad.

00:35:24.889 --> 00:35:27.323
I'm flying.

00:35:30.394 --> 00:35:31.588
Bad landing.

00:35:31.662 --> 00:35:33.994
Call the MedEvac team.

00:35:35.900 --> 00:35:38.061
Paris to Seven.
Status?

00:35:38.136 --> 00:35:40.661
I've located the device,
but it's fused to the hull.

00:35:40.738 --> 00:35:41.830
Stand by.

00:35:41.906 --> 00:35:42.930
Make it quick.

00:35:43.074 --> 00:35:44.598
We've got less than 15 minutes.

00:35:44.675 --> 00:35:46.939
Understood.

00:35:50.515 --> 00:35:53.313
All systems go.

00:35:53.384 --> 00:35:56.785
Watch me, Dad. I'm flying.

00:36:00.525 --> 00:36:02.288
Bad landing.

00:36:02.360 --> 00:36:04.624
Call the MedEvac team.

00:36:07.698 --> 00:36:09.666
John Kelly's first flight...

00:36:09.734 --> 00:36:12.134
not exactly A-okay.

00:36:12.203 --> 00:36:14.228
Remember that, Dad?

00:36:14.305 --> 00:36:16.603
I jumped off the roof

00:36:16.674 --> 00:36:18.642
with a parachute
made out of blankets.

00:36:20.545 --> 00:36:24.311
I guess I didn't calculate
the aerodynamics.

00:36:24.382 --> 00:36:26.373
Of course...

00:36:26.450 --> 00:36:28.441
I was only six.

00:36:28.519 --> 00:36:33.786
I guess this is
John Kelly's last flight.

00:36:33.858 --> 00:36:37.919
This time
I can't blame it on pilot error.

00:36:38.062 --> 00:36:41.122
This time... no regrets.

00:36:41.199 --> 00:36:45.829
What I've seen... proves...

00:36:45.903 --> 00:36:48.303
we were right to come out here.

00:36:48.372 --> 00:36:50.499
We're not alone.

00:36:50.575 --> 00:36:52.406
I know that now.

00:36:54.679 --> 00:36:56.943
The module's losing power.

00:36:59.016 --> 00:37:02.042
I'm taking life support
off-line.

00:37:02.119 --> 00:37:05.885
Rerouting whatever's left
to the imager.

00:37:07.658 --> 00:37:10.991
Keep it running...
as long as possible.

00:37:13.030 --> 00:37:15.794
Mission control...

00:37:15.866 --> 00:37:18.130
Dad...

00:37:18.202 --> 00:37:20.363
whoever finds this...

00:37:21.872 --> 00:37:23.806
do me a favor.

00:37:26.544 --> 00:37:28.569
Take all the data
I've collected...

00:37:30.047 --> 00:37:31.844
put it to good use.

00:37:34.051 --> 00:37:36.815
I hope you don't look at this
as a failure.

00:37:38.723 --> 00:37:40.588
I don't.

00:37:40.658 --> 00:37:42.922
Actually...

00:37:42.994 --> 00:37:44.757
I do have one regret.

00:37:44.829 --> 00:37:47.627
I never found out...

00:37:47.698 --> 00:37:49.563
who won the World Series.

00:37:54.171 --> 00:37:56.605
I'm tired.

00:37:56.674 --> 00:37:58.266
I can't...

00:38:16.327 --> 00:38:17.624
Paris to Seven.

00:38:17.695 --> 00:38:19.629
How's it going over there?

00:38:19.697 --> 00:38:21.130
I have the distributor.

00:38:21.198 --> 00:38:23.291
I'm downloading
Lieutenant Kelly's database.

00:38:23.367 --> 00:38:26.393
Hurry, we're running out
of time.

00:38:46.357 --> 00:38:48.552
Lock onto my bio-signature
and my combadge.

00:38:48.626 --> 00:38:49.888
Seven?

00:38:49.960 --> 00:38:51.086
Energize.

00:39:07.511 --> 00:39:08.773
Delta Flyer, report.

00:39:08.846 --> 00:39:10.837
We're trying to integrate
the distributor.

00:39:10.915 --> 00:39:12.041
Stand by, Captain.

00:39:12.116 --> 00:39:13.378
How long do they have?

00:39:13.451 --> 00:39:15.112
Four minutes.

00:39:17.088 --> 00:39:19.283
Try bypassing
the power couplings.

00:39:19.357 --> 00:39:20.984
No effect.

00:39:21.058 --> 00:39:24.994
Resequence the ion modulators;
see if that does it.

00:39:26.364 --> 00:39:29.527
Power conversion
in progress.

00:39:29.600 --> 00:39:33.297
We have propulsion, shields.

00:39:33.371 --> 00:39:36.135
The plasma flow
is still fluctuating.

00:39:36.207 --> 00:39:37.799
Stay on top of it.

00:39:37.875 --> 00:39:39.308
Paris to Voyager.

00:39:39.377 --> 00:39:40.901
Go ahead.

00:39:41.045 --> 00:39:43.309
Open the Shuttle Bay doors;
we're coming home.

00:39:43.381 --> 00:39:45.315
Acknowledged.

00:39:45.383 --> 00:39:47.510
Laying in an escape trajectory.

00:39:53.557 --> 00:39:55.115
The ellipse
is returning to subspace.

00:39:55.192 --> 00:39:56.625
The Flyer.

00:39:56.694 --> 00:39:58.457
They're approaching
the perimeter...

00:39:58.529 --> 00:40:01.293
2,000 meters... 1,800.

00:40:01.365 --> 00:40:02.855
The anomaly's submerging.

00:40:05.269 --> 00:40:06.361
Are we in tractor range?

00:40:06.437 --> 00:40:07.369
Not yet.

00:40:07.438 --> 00:40:08.370
Take us closer.

00:40:08.439 --> 00:40:09.371
Captain?

00:40:09.440 --> 00:40:10.429
Do it.

00:40:10.508 --> 00:40:11.532
They are still out of reach.

00:40:11.609 --> 00:40:12.541
Another 300 meters.

00:40:12.610 --> 00:40:13.577
Closer.

00:40:13.644 --> 00:40:14.941
We'll be pulled
right in with them.

00:40:15.045 --> 00:40:16.034
Just a few more meters.

00:40:19.116 --> 00:40:20.947
We're too close!

00:40:21.085 --> 00:40:22.347
I've got a lock.

00:40:22.420 --> 00:40:24.820
Reverse thrusters, full impulse.

00:40:48.212 --> 00:40:52.148
Captain's Log,
Stardate 53301.2.

00:40:52.216 --> 00:40:53.513
The away team collected

00:40:53.584 --> 00:40:56.382
over 60 teraquads of data
on the anomaly.

00:40:56.454 --> 00:40:58.422
Before we begin
to analyze them,

00:40:58.489 --> 00:41:00.923
we've decided
to pay our respects

00:41:00.991 --> 00:41:01.923
to an old colleague.

00:41:01.992 --> 00:41:04.153
Space...

00:41:04.228 --> 00:41:07.322
literally, it means "nothing"...

00:41:07.398 --> 00:41:10.629
a vacuum
between stars and planets.

00:41:10.701 --> 00:41:13.135
But by the same token,
it means everything.

00:41:13.204 --> 00:41:15.934
It's what connects
all our worlds...

00:41:16.040 --> 00:41:18.270
Vulcan, Qo'noS,

00:41:18.342 --> 00:41:21.072
Talax, Earth.

00:41:22.346 --> 00:41:24.280
Centuries ago,

00:41:24.348 --> 00:41:28.614
mankind sent its first wave
of explorers into that void.

00:41:28.686 --> 00:41:31.177
Astronauts like Mr. Kelly.

00:41:31.255 --> 00:41:34.622
They paved the way
for the first colonies,

00:41:34.692 --> 00:41:36.626
the first starships.

00:41:36.694 --> 00:41:39.492
For those of us
who've made space our home,

00:41:41.198 --> 00:41:44.133
We commend
the spirit and the bravery

00:41:44.201 --> 00:41:46.897
of Lieutenant John Mark Kelly

00:41:46.971 --> 00:41:49.235
as we commit his body...

00:41:49.306 --> 00:41:51.570
to space.

00:41:51.642 --> 00:41:53.872
He will not be forgotten.

00:41:53.944 --> 00:41:56.208
Captain...

00:42:00.317 --> 00:42:03.218
I did not know this individual.

00:42:03.287 --> 00:42:06.222
Had I encountered him
while I was a Borg,

00:42:06.290 --> 00:42:08.622
I would have found
his technology

00:42:08.692 --> 00:42:10.387
unworthy of assimilation...

00:42:12.663 --> 00:42:15.928
but we are more alike
than one might think.

00:42:16.066 --> 00:42:19.661
In a sense,
his desire to explore

00:42:19.737 --> 00:42:22.763
was not unlike
a quest for perfection.

00:42:22.840 --> 00:42:26.936
His contribution
helped secure humanity's future,

00:42:27.044 --> 00:42:30.241
and in some ways, my own.

00:42:33.551 --> 00:42:36.611
The Yankees...

00:42:36.687 --> 00:42:39.019
in six games.

00:42:45.262 --> 00:42:47.992
All hands, attention.

00:42:52.169 --> 00:42:53.966
Honor guard.
